WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.440
Input validation is the practice of checking whether data is acceptable before an application trusts it or processes it.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.420
It answers a practical question: does this value match what the application expects at this point in the workflow?

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.680
A valid value should have the right type, format, length, range, and relationship to the business rule it is meant to support.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.660
Validation is related to, but different from, several other controls.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.680
Sanitization attempts to clean or transform data.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.340
Normalization converts data into a consistent representation.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.280
Parsing turns raw input into a structured form the application understands.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:44.540
Output encoding prepares data safely for a specific output context, such as HTML, JavaScript, SQL parameters, logs, or command arguments.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.000
These controls often work together, but they are not interchangeable.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:58.340
External input must be treated as untrusted because it can be malformed, unexpected, hostile, stale, or outside the assumptions made by the application.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:06.880
A request may come from a browser, a script, an API client, a partner integration, an imported file, or a compromised internal system.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.860
The application should not assume that user interface controls are the only path into server-side logic.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.920
Input validation is one layer of defense, not the whole security model.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:29.300
It helps make application behavior safer and more predictable, but it does not replace authorization, safe APIs, parameterized queries, output encoding, secure file handling, business logic checks, or careful design.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:36.080
Strong applications validate early, enforce on the server, and still use context-appropriate controls at every boundary.
